WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.300
This is the Microsoft for Education Daily, for Thursday.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.180
Here's your day in Microsoft updates, in 3 minutes flat.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.130
Less guesswork for students: when a formula breaks,

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.020
Excel on Windows now explains what went wrong.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.950
Hover over the error for causes and fixes, or let Copilot repair it.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.950
Lecture theatre with two screens?

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:38.620
Teams Rooms on Android will show your shared laptop on both, due January.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:44.580
Plug in over HDMI and the back row sees it too, even outside a Teams meeting.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.790
Opened a read-only report from the office?

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:52.260
Copilot in Excel now works there too, rolling out.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.960
Student coders? This week's VS Code starts chat answers about 30% faster.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:00.800
School IT?

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.210
Microsoft 365 Backup now keeps OneDrive and SharePoint backups up to ten years.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.300
The default stays one year,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.970
single-file restores only go back 14 days, and Exchange stops at two.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:15.720
Due January,

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.720
Teams admins should see which outside bots turned up in their meetings and events.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.680
And SharePoint gets organization-wide page templates,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.410
due December: approved starting points for school news pages.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.270
Something calmer for coding classes: when two docs pages disagree,

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.720
AI agents often follow the wrong one.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.220
In Microsoft's test, agents that checked a second source weren't misled at all.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.020
Good advice for students too.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:45.910
The fine print?

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:52.080
November's Windows security updates can break sign-ins through some proxies and single sign-on tools.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.360
And Microsoft warns attackers are abusing ARTEX,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.760
an AI pen-testing tool that can break in within minutes.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:02.760
After hours,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.410
good news for 8 GB laptops: Microsoft says its own apps now use less memory.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:16.320
Office uses 360 MB less; Defender, Purview and OneDrive save over half a gig.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.850
On Xbox, a Brazilian player is the first to hit 10 million Gamerscore,

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:22.980
after 20 years.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.050
Clearer errors, lighter laptops,

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:29.460
longer backups: a little less guesswork all round.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.020
Day after day, the updates keep stacking up.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.620
Some of it's shipped. The rest is on its way.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.880
One day, all of this. Tomorrow, probably more.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.720
This has been the Microsoft for Education Daily.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:49.940
See you tomorrow.
